Q: The &lt;map&gt; tag on the image does not work if it is loaded dynamically rather than directly in the HTML code A: <p>The inconsistent behavior of your image map when loaded dynamically is primarily due to two issues:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Malformed HTML String:</strong> Your <code>imagemapElement</code> string contains invalid HTML. Specifically:
<ul>
<li><code>alt=&quot;'&quot;</code>: The <code>alt</code> attribute for the <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> is incorrectly terminated, leading to parsing errors.</li>
<li>Extra <code>&lt;/div&gt;</code>: You have an unmatched <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> tag in your concatenated string, which can break the DOM structure and prevent the browser from correctly associating the <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> with its <code>usemap</code>.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Incorrect DOM Manipulation:</strong>
<ul>
<li>The <code>div</code> variable used in <code>div.innerHTML</code> and <code>imagemap.appendChild(div)</code> is undefined.</li>
<li>Using <code>innerHTML</code> to set content and then attempting <code>appendChild</code> with an undefined variable is redundant and erroneous.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<p>When the browser encounters malformed HTML, especially with elements like <code>&lt;map&gt;</code> that rely on specific attributes (<code>name</code>) and their association with other elements (<code>&lt;img&gt;</code>'s <code>usemap</code>), it may fail to parse them correctly or create an unexpected DOM structure. This leads to the <code>area</code> coordinates being misaligned or completely ignored for parts of the image.</p>
<h3>Corrected JavaScript</h3>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// 1. Get the target container element
var imagemapContainer = document.getElementById('imagemap');

if (imagemapContainer) {
    // 2. Construct a valid HTML string for the image and map
    //    - Corrected alt=&quot;&quot;
    //    - Removed extra &lt;div&gt; tags
    var validHtmlContent = `
        &lt;img src=&quot;image.png&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; usemap=&quot;#mymap&quot;&gt;
        &lt;map name=&quot;mymap&quot;&gt;
            &lt;area shape=&quot;rect&quot; coords=&quot;0,0,100,100&quot; href=&quot;#&quot; alt=&quot;A&quot; onclick=&quot;handleAreaClick()&quot;&gt;
        &lt;/map&gt;
    `;

    // 3. Set the innerHTML of the container
    imagemapContainer.innerHTML = validHtmlContent;

    // 4. (Optional) Apply styling if needed
    // imagemapContainer.style.display = &quot;inline&quot;;
}

// 5. Define your event handler function
//    NOTE: Avoid using 'try' as a function name; it's a reserved JavaScript keyword.
function handleAreaClick() {
    console.log(&quot;Image map area clicked!&quot;);
    // Add your specific logic here
}
</code></pre>
<p>By providing valid HTML and correctly updating the DOM, the browser can properly establish the link between the <code>&lt;img&gt;</code>'s <code>usemap</code> attribute and the <code>&lt;map&gt;</code> element's <code>name</code> attribute, ensuring the image map areas function as expected.</p>

Q: Bash: accessing a large array at random indices is slow A: <p>Despite Bash (executable <code>bash</code>) being indeed the wrong tool for the job (linked lists… see comments above), it is still possible to do this with acceptable performance, using <a href="https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Coprocesses.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">a coprocess</a> and <a href="https://linux.die.net/man/1/shuf" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>shuf -r</code></a>.</p>
<p>A bit elaborated example that requires <code>$a</code> from above:</p>
<pre><code>(
     shopt -s lastpipe
     coproc rid {
         for e in &quot;${a[@]}&quot;; do
             echo &quot;$e&quot;;
         done | shuf -r;
     };
     seq 150000 \
     | while read s; do
         for i in {1..10}; do
             read -u ${rid[0]} a;
             s=&quot;$s:$a&quot;;
         done;
         echo &quot;$s&quot;;
     done
) | pv -ls 150000 &gt;/dev/null
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li><p>The <code>-r</code> gets <code>shuf</code> to not shuffle, but it just spits out random elements without remembering indefinitely.</p>
</li>
<li><p>The outer <code>( )</code> is necessary, because I wanted the output of the loop to go to <code>pv</code> for measuring. This shows a problem I have with this solution: I don't think coprocesses are easy to use, since you cannot read their output from a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Child_process#Children_created_by_fork" rel="nofollow noreferrer">subshell</a>, and you have to think about how to terminate them cleanly. <code>shopt -s lastpipe</code> may help with some problems.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>All in all, this is about 200 times faster than the original version (take this with more than one grain of salt), but of course it is still a similar factor slower than an optimized native implementation.</p>
